We've actually had a lot of discussion about this case. As Marcus pointed out, the limit behavior as y goes to +/- infinity is well defined.

Matt has mentioned in the past that the quantity that really matters is y-mu, and I agree. Perhaps we should be checking that y-mu is not nan, but at the time of implementation, we left the checks as is.

I would rather not throw exceptions when the behavior is well defined. There may be cases where the returned value exp transformed. If we threw an exception, that sort of code would not have the correct behavior.

