> > > > > > >                         &logp, Policy()))
> > > > > > >         return logp;
> > > > > > >
> > Why do we check that y is not NaN but do not check that it is finite, or equivalently, why don't we check that y is finite which includes the case where some of its elements are NaN? I can imagine situations where we would want to check whether a parameter is finite, and if it is not finite and not NaN, do some limiting thing. But for a random variable, I can't think of an example where infinity is okay.
