It's going to return a -infinity value.  The alternative
is to throw an exception.  So it gets back to the earlier
message.

There seem to be lots of opinions on this.

I think Boost raises domain errors for parameters out of
domain and returns 0 for infinite variates.

And Matt was arguing for symmetry of location parameter
and variate.

I don't really care much what we do as long as we try
to do it consistently.  My own preference is tightly
controlling domains and throwing errors as early as
possible unless there are finite limiting cases.

I'm not even sure what to do in some limiting cases.
If the scale of a normal goes to +infinity, do we
want to treat the whole thing like an improper uniform
distribution?  But then what if the variate is infinite?

