I've not been able to find anything that comes out and enthusiastically says algorithms like his 1988 algorithm are okay for statistical computing. L'Ecuyer's most recent survey

http://scholar.google.com/scholar?cluster=16324058590511781724&hl=en&as_sdt=0,33

says that the period should be much bigger than the square of the number of pseudo-random numbers you need. I guess that depends on the number of parameters in our case. Is it conceivable that we would need 2 billion? In the last section, he says "Computer implementations that this author can suggest for the moment include ... " a few of his from the 1990s, the Mersenne-Twister ones, and a Fortran one.

Still don't really know what to make of all this.

Ben

