On my Mac:

   2.3 GHz i7, 16GB 1600MHz DDR3, Macbook Pro, OSX 10.8.2 (Lion)

with latest g++ and clang++ directly from latest Xcode (4.5.1):

   g++:
   i686-apple-darwin11-llvm-g++-4.2 (GCC) 4.2.1
   (Based on Apple Inc. build 5658) (LLVM build 2336.11.00)

   clang++:
   Apple clang version 4.1 (tags/Apple/clang-421.11.65)
   (based on LLVM 3.1svn)

Has g++ always been based on LLVM or has this changed?

For BUGS Vol 1 Rats model, using

   make clean-all; make -j4 CC= O=3 models/src/bugs/vol1/rats/rats

(The just model time I touched the model and reran.)

The make and run time for 100K iterations are:

clang++
--------

make all:        28s
make model:       9s
run 100K iter:   13s

g++ (4.2.1)
-------
make all:      1m23s
make model:      10s
run 100K iter:   13s

So it looks like Ben's right, at least for the latest g++ and
clang++ from Xcode.

You can get later versions of g++ from Macports.  I don't know
about later versions of clang++.

- Bob

