At this point, it is easier to benchmark rstan. I made a rstan package from master after setting g++ in $HOME/.R/Makevars and did

example(stan_demo)

bench <- function(i) {
  compile <- system.time(fit <- stan_demo(i, chains = 0, iter = 0))
  execute <- system.time(fit <- stan_demo(i, fit = fit, seed = 12345, refresh = -1))
  if(length(dim(fit)) == 0) execute <- NA_real_ # failure
  return(rbind(compile, execute))
}

results <- mclapply(1:length(model_names), mc.cores = 4L, FUN = bench,
                    mc.preschedule = FALSE)

names(results) <- model_names

then I saved those results, recompiled a rstan package after setting clang++ in $HOME/.R/Makevars, and did the above again. Over about 75 models, I would declare a tie on my laptop on the basis of the attachment.

