 On Monday, October 22, 2012 1:38:09 AM UTC-4, Bob Carpenter wrote:
> Was that at -O3 optimization?

Yes, specifically just varying CC and CXX before building rstan:

goodrich@CYBERPOWERPC:~$ cat ~/.R/Makevars
CC=clang
CXX=clang++

# created by rstan at Fri Oct 12 00:16:32 2012
CXXFLAGS = -O3 -pipe  $(LTO)  #set_by_rstan
R_XTRA_CPPFLAGS =  -I$(R_INCLUDE_DIR) -DNDEBUG  #set_by_rstan
goodrich@CYBERPOWERPC:~$

> And is that an LLVM-based gcc or front-to-back
> gcc?  (Or does that quesiton even make sense on
> Linux.)

You could probably accomplish that on Linux, but I've never done it. Clang was using the GNU C++ standard library libstdc++. The Clang people have their version of the standard library

http://libcxx.llvm.org/

but Stan doesn't work with that yet. Actually, it might work but gtest doesn't, so Stan has never passed test-unit linking against it.

> Although not ideal for testing compilers, if
> you run under R, it makes sense to test there.
> It also makes sense to throw in compile time as far
> as that goes, I think.

The compile time was like 5x longer for g++, but we knew that already.

Ben

