n 10/23/12 1:32 AM, Ben Goodrich wrote:
> Would it make sense to have init default to 2 and draw initial values from the (-init, init) interval? That would make
> it easy to get initial values from a wider or narrow interval and still preserve the existing behavior when init is zero.

Yes, this would be very easy.

It would also be easy to sample from a distribution
as Marcus suggested at some point.

It's all in the stan/gm/command.hpp code, starting
around line 351:

         init_val = "random initialization";  // for I/O
         // init_rng generates uniformly from -2 to 2
         boost::random::uniform_real_distribution<double>
           init_range_distribution(-2.0,2.0);
         boost::variate_generator<rng_t&,
                        boost::random::uniform_real_distribution<double> >
           init_rng(base_rng,init_range_distribution);

         params_i = std::vector<int>(model.num_params_i(),0);
         params_r = std::vector<double>(model.num_params_r());

         // retry inits until get a finite log prob value
         std::vector<double> init_grad;
         static int MAX_INIT_TRIES = 100;
         for (num_init_tries = 1; num_init_tries <= MAX_INIT_TRIES; ++num_init_tries) {
           for (size_t i = 0; i < params_r.size(); ++i)
             params_r[i] = init_rng();

All you need to do is extract the init value from the command
line and give it a default, then use it instead of 2.0 in
the above.  Using a different distribution other than uniform's
also easy -- that's just the init_range_distribution spec.

- Bob

