On 10/28/12 9:45 PM, Ben Goodrich wrote:
> On Sunday, October 28, 2012 3:01:01 AM UTC-4, Bob Carpenter wrote:
>
>           template <int R, int C>
>           VV(const Eigen::Matrix<scalar_t,R,C>& m) : x_(&m(0)) { }
>
>
> I don't know what would happen if m were an Eigen::DiagonalMatrix.

C++ would happily convert the Eigen::DiagonalMatrix to an
Eigen::Matrix by filling in the zeroes implicit in the
diagonal matrix.  So it's very wasteful.

It does have the advantage of providing the right semantics.
If you take mean(m), you want to count those zeroes!

 > But I also can't think of a case where we would want
> to make a VV out of a DiagonalMatrix.

The thing to do would be to pull out the diag and use that:

    VV(diag(m));

> I guess we might as well make a constructor for an Eigen::Array also, although I
> guess that would only be relevant for now if someone were using C++ directly.

That makes sense.

I've just been lazy in only adding the operations we need.
(One advantage of that is that it makes the code easier to
read, test, and therefore maintain;  the disadvantage is that
things that "should" work won't.)

- Bob

