> >     I guess we have a few options here. But I still think that the range bounds in users' models should always be
> >     checked on the first iteration only. I guess that would entail passing the iteration number to log_prob or defining
> >     a bool in the class or something.
>
> I'm afraid we need to keep the range checks in place
> all the time by default.  Otherwise, there's no way to
> stop segfaults from taking down R or the command.
>
> Stan is not like JAGS or BUGS where the loops are all
> determined when the data is read in.  In Stan, loops can
> have bounds that depend on random quantities being sampled:
>
>   for (n in 1:floor(Z)) { ... }
>
> I'm OK with a power-user mode that turns all the checking
> off.  I think that'll be easier to write and understand,
> too.
>
> Then we just follow Matt's water-wing metaphor, which,
> language being a virus, is now attached lamprey-like to
> my poor mind.  (And no, mailer, I'm not going to attach
> a lamprey to the message, thank you very much.)

On a side note, is it possible to fork() and trap the segfault when it's run in R?  Or run the model in a separate process with an exec() call?  These kinds of bugs are likely to be a semi-recurring issue (because, lets be honest, bugs happen and users will always find creative ways to build models which crash) and it'd be nice if it just printed a sufficiently ominous error rather than take down R.  I'm not sure if this has been discussed before, so forgive me if it's come up and been dismissed for whatever reason.

Cheers,
Marcus

